<section class="case-section alt">
    <div class="case-container">
        <div class="case-section-header">
            <p class="section-eyebrow">ENGINEERING TROUBLESHOOTING</p>
            <h2>Duplicate IP &amp; ARP Conflict</h2>
            <p>
                AUTOMATION01 could reach its local management gateway but could not reach
                Splunk across the routed network. The actual cause was a duplicate IP conflict
                on the management network rather than a pfSense routing failure.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="case-grid">
            <article class="case-card">
                <h3>Symptom</h3>
                <p>n8n HEC requests timed out even though the automation host had a valid default route.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="case-card">
                <h3>Diagnosis</h3>
                <p>The ARP neighbor entry for the gateway showed an unexpected VMware MAC address.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="case-card">
                <h3>Root Cause</h3>
                <p>A Windows VMware host-only adapter also used <strong>172.16.30.1</strong>, conflicting with pfSense MANAGEMENT.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="case-card">
                <h3>Fix</h3>
                <p>The conflicting host adapter address was corrected and the ARP cache was flushed.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="case-card">
                <h3>Validation</h3>
                <p>The gateway MAC changed to pfSense and cross-subnet connectivity to Splunk was restored.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="case-card">
                <h3>Final Recovery</h3>
                <p>After network recovery and HEC authorization correction, HIGH, MEDIUM, and LOW incidents completed end to end.</p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

